Which technique does CSMA/CA use to avoid collisions?
 a: Carrier Sense and Random Backoff

Why does CSMA/CA use an acknowledgment (ACK) mechanism?
 a: To confirm successful data transmission and avoid retransmission

What is the primary reason CSMA/CA is used in wireless networks instead of CSMA/CD?
 a: Wireless networks have higher transmission speeds

What is the purpose of the RTS/CTS mechanism in CSMA/CA?
 a: To reduce the hidden node problem
What happens when multiple devices sense an idle channel in CSMA/CA?
b: Devices use a random backoff timer before transmitting
Why is CSMA/CA preferred in Wi-Fi networks?
 a: Because Wi-Fi signals are prone to interference and hidden nodes
How does the backoff mechanism in CSMA/CA help in avoiding collisions?
  b: It introduces a random delay before retransmission
What does the Clear-to-Send (CTS) signal indicate in CSMA/CA?
 b: The sender can now transmit data
What happens when a device detects that the channel is busy in CSMA/CA?
 c: It defers transmission and starts a backoff timer
If two devices transmit at the same time despite CSMA/CA, what happens?
c: Packets may collide and require retransmission
How does CSMA/CA handle the hidden node problem?
 a: By using RTS/CTS before transmitting data
Which of the following best explains why CSMA/CD is suitable for wired Ethernet but not for wireless networks (where CSMA/CA is used)?
c: In wireless, a station cannot 'listen' for a collision while transmitting because its own signal strength is much higher than any incoming signal.

